      commentary: 'The largest of the ancient Upanishads opens, like the Mandukya, with a single syllable — but from a different door. Here Om is approached as the Udgītha, the soaring central chant of the Sama Veda, the sung heart of the ritual. The Chandogya belongs to the Sama Veda, the Veda of melody, and so it begins not with silence but with song: meditate on Om as the chant, for every chant begins with it.\n\nBeneath the ritual framing is the same recognition the whole tradition keeps circling. The chapter goes on to call Om the rasatama, the "essence of essences" — the earth\'s essence is water, water\'s essence is plants, the plant\'s essence is the human being, the human essence is speech, speech\'s essence is the sacred chant, and the essence of that is Om. Layer within layer, the text distills all of manifest reality down to a single sound, and that sound to the imperishable behind it. The word akṣara does its usual double work: syllable, and the imperishable.\n\nThe Chandogya will climb, over eight chapters, to the highest reaches of Indian thought — "That thou art," "All this is Brahman," the space within the heart. But it starts where its own Veda lives, in the sung syllable, teaching that the way to the imperishable can begin in something as simple and embodied as a chant rightly attended. Sing Om knowing what it is, and the singing itself becomes a ladder.'
