      commentary: 'Śvetaketu has come home from twelve years of study puffed up with learning, and his father Uddālaka sets out to teach him the one thing all that learning missed. He hands him a lump of salt: put it in water overnight. In the morning: "Bring me the salt." The boy reaches into the water and finds nothing — the salt has vanished. "Taste it from the top," says the father. "Salt." "From the middle." "Salt." "From the bottom." "Salt." The salt is gone from sight, yet it is wholly present, in every drop, evenly, invisibly.\n\n"You do not see Being here, my dear," Uddālaka says, "and yet it is right here." That is the whole teaching in an image a child could hold. The subtle essence, Sat, the one Being, is not visible the way objects are — you cannot pluck it out and point to it — and yet it saturates everything, is present in every part of everything, the way dissolved salt is in every drop of the water. Look for it as a thing among things and you find nothing; taste anywhere and it is all you find.\n\nAnd then the line the entire chapter has been building toward: "That which is the subtle essence — this whole world has that as its Self. That is the real. That is the Self. And that thou art, Śvetaketu." The salt in the water is the answer to the boy\'s pride: reality is not something you can grasp and possess with learning, because it is not an object at all. It is the invisible fullness in which everything is steeped — including the one reaching in to find it.'
