      commentary: 'This verse opens the Upanishad\'s final and most story-like teaching — Prajāpati\'s instruction to Indra, king of the gods, and Virochana, king of the demons. It begins by announcing the goal in a cascade of freedoms: the Self is untouched by evil, unaging, undying, sorrowless, beyond hunger and thirst; its desires and its will are satya — real, true, self-fulfilling. This is worth seeking; this is worth longing to know.\n\nWhat follows is one of the most psychologically astute passages in all of scripture. Indra and Virochana come to Prajāpati and are given, at first, a deliberately incomplete answer: "The self you see reflected in water or a mirror — that is the Self." Virochana, satisfied, goes home and teaches his demons that the body is the Self — pamper it, adorn it, and you have everything. (The text is quietly saying: materialism is the demons\' religion.) But Indra, on the way home, sees the flaw — if the Self is the body\'s reflection, then it grows old and dies with the body — and returns, again and again, through deeper and deeper answers: the dream-self, the deep-sleep self, and finally the true Self beyond all three states, the very awareness for which the whole teaching was given.\n\nThe genius of the story is that both students hear the same words and receive utterly different teachings, because one stopped at the first satisfying answer and the other kept returning, unsatisfied, until he reached the truth. It is a portrait of the seeker\'s essential virtue: not cleverness, but the refusal to settle — the willingness to come back to the teacher a fourth and a fifth time, saying "there is still a difficulty here." The deathless, sorrowless Self named in this opening verse is real, and available — but only to the one who, like Indra, will not mistake the reflection for the reality, and will not stop until the true Self is found.'
